<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking 
<br class="ltx_break">Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chunsan Hong
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Graduate School of AI, KAIST
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Seonho An
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computing, KAIST
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Min-Soo Kim
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computing, KAIST
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jong Chul Ye
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Graduate School of AI, KAIST
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Masked diffusion models (MDMs) have recently emerged as a novel framework for language modeling. MDMs generate sentences by iteratively denoising masked sequences, filling in [MASK] tokens step by step.
Although MDMs support any-order sampling, performance is highly sensitive to the choice of <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which position to unmask next</em>. Prior work typically relies on rule-based schedules (e.g., max-confidence, max-margin), which provide ad hoc improvements. In contrast, we replace these heuristics with a <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learned</em> scheduler.
Specifically, we cast denoising as a KL–regularized Markov decision process (MDP) with an explicit reference policy and optimize a regularized objective that admits policy-improvement and convergence guarantees under standard assumptions.
We prove that the optimized policy under this framework generates samples that more closely match the data distribution than heuristic schedules.
Empirically, across four benchmarks, our learned policy consistently outperforms max-confidence: for example, on <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span>, where unmasking order is critical, it yields a 20.1% gain over random and a 11.2% gain over max-confidence.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have achieved remarkable success in domains such as image generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, by defining a noise-adding forward process and a denoising reverse process in continuous space, and learning the reverse dynamics within this framework. Recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have advanced this line of work into the discrete domain, applying it to language modeling through masked diffusion models (MDMs).
In the discrete setting, MDMs define a forward process that replaces tokens in text with a [<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MASK</span>] token and a corresponding reverse process; training typically maximizes an evidence lower bound (ELBO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to fit the reverse dynamics. Small-scale MDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have matched or surpassed autoregressive models (ARMs), and recent large-scale studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> report results competitive with strong ARMs such as LLaMA-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite a shared template, diffusion in discrete versus continuous spaces differs substantially—especially at inference. Continuous-space diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2022</a>)</cite> perform denoising by integrating a stochastic differential equation (SDE) or an ordinary differential equation (ODE). In contrast, discrete-space diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> denoise by iteratively sampling concrete replacements for [<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MASK</span>] tokens. These mechanisms lead to different update geometries: continuous models accumulate numerous small changes across all dimensions, whereas discrete models enact localized, high-magnitude jumps at a few coordinates. Consequently, effective denoising in discrete space requires its own methodology. Just as advances in SDE solvers unlocked large gains for continuous diffusion, improved denoising strategies are likely to yield substantial performance gains for MDMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This raises a natural question: what matters most in MDM denoising? Among several factors, we focus on the choice of <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which position to unmask next</em>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite> provide a theoretical rationale for this choice, proving that no polynomial-time algorithm can solve any-order generation; <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, we cannot train MDMs that exactly recover the real data distribution for all masked sentences. However, they empirically show that selecting unmasking tokens via max-confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> or max-margin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite> at inference time can bypass sub-hard instances and even outperform ARMs. Reflecting this, contemporary large-scale MDMs such as LLaDA or Dream-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite> commonly adopt max-confidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we aim to move beyond heuristic references (e.g., max-confidence) by <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learning</em> improved unmasking policies. To this end, we cast MDM denoising as a Markov decision process (MDP) and train an unmasking position-selection policy using the group relative policy optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Theoretically, we prove that under this framework, the optimized policy produces samples closer to the true data distribution than the strong heuristic references. Moreover, we introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">three</span> tractable surrogate objectives and provide propositions that justify optimizing them in place of the ideal, yet intractable, objective. Practically, our learned policy substitutes for max-confidence and related heuristics, achieving improved accuracy across four benchmarks. For example, on <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span>, where unmasking order is crucial, it delivers a 20.1% improvement over random and a 11.2% improvement over max-confidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related works.</span>
Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as an effective tool to fine-tune large language models toward complex objectives or preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In MDMs, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zekri &amp; Boullé (2025)</a></cite> apply policy gradient methods to a pretrained discrete diffusion model so as to maximize a task-specific reward at the last denoising step. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite> adopts the GRPO framework to improve the reasoning ability by rewarding only the final answer accuracy. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose a variance reduced preference optimization (VRPO) for MDMs, which shares the concept of direct preference optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite> in ARMs. While the aforementioned works utilize RL to post-train the MDM itself, they differ from ours, where we train the unmasking policy model.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DColT</span>, which also trains an unmasking policy via reinforcement learning. Our approach differs in that we formulate denoising as a KL–regularized MDP with an explicit <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reference policy</em> and optimize a regularized objective that admits policy-improvement and convergence guarantees under standard assumptions.
Furthermore, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> jointly applies RL to train both the unmasking policy and the underlying MDMs for maximum accuracy, our work focuses solely on optimizing the unmasking policy for frozen MDMs, thereby reducing computational cost and mitigating the risk of reward over-optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminary</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notations.</span>
Let the distribution <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the data distribution over sequences of length <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with vocabulary <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}=\{1,...,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}=\{1,...,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let the set of all possible sequences be <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{V}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{V}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the Kronecker delta distribution that assigns all probability mass to the sentence in which every token is masked.
We denote the kernel forms of <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{P}}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{P}}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, with entries <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}]{{\bm{x}}}=p_{\rm{data}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}]{{\bm{x}}}=p_{\rm{data}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}=\{x_{n}^{1},x_{n}^{2},\dots,x_{n}^{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}=\{x_{n}^{1},x_{n}^{2},\dots,x_{n}^{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th step sequence with <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. We denote the mask by <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and the set of sequences with <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> masks by <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{n}=\{{\bm{x}}|{\bm{x}}\in\{\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbb{M}\}\}^{L},\sum_{i=1}^{L}{\textbf{1}(x^{i}=\mathbb{M})}=n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{n}=\{{\bm{x}}|{\bm{x}}\in\{\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbb{M}\}\}^{L},\sum_{i=1}^{L}{\textbf{1}(x^{i}=\mathbb{M})}=n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}\in\mathcal{X}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}\in\mathcal{X}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> masks and
we refer <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th mask index of <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, if the number of masks in <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]\in\{1,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]\in\{1,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]}=\mathbb{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]}=\mathbb{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For brevity, we will omit <math id="S2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{i}[{\bm{x}}]</annotation></semantics></math> for the rest of the paper.
We denote MDMs as <math id="S2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and their model distribution as <math id="S2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote Markov transition kernel <math id="S2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability of <math id="S2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> transform into <math id="S2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum{\mathbf{T}}_{{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum{\mathbf{T}}_{{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Masked diffusion models.</span>
Masked Diffusion Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> define a mask-adding forward process and corresponding reverse process in discrete space, and learn the model <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to mimic the true reverse process. The forward process gradually masks tokens independently one-by-one in <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> until the sequence is fully masked at <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=L</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t:=n/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t:=n/L</annotation></semantics></math>). For <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=n/L\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=n/L\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the
sequence <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is partially masked, with each being masked with probability <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> or remaining unmasked with probability <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-t</annotation></semantics></math>. The reverse process recovers the data distribution by iteratively predicting masked tokens as <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> decreases from <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. While previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> propose the time-dependent MDM training framework, the recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> propose the time-agnostic model where time is implicitly included in the number of masks. Then, the time-agnostic loss for MDM is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}(\theta)\triangleq-\mathbb{E}_{n,{\bm{x}}_{0},{\bm{x}}_{n}}\big[\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{L}\textbf{1}[x_{n}^{i}=\mathbb{M}]\log\pi_{\theta}(x_{0}^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})\big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MDM</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≜</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}(\theta)\triangleq-\mathbb{E}_{n,{\bm{x}}_{0},{\bm{x}}_{n}}\big[\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{L}\textbf{1}[x_{n}^{i}=\mathbb{M}]\log\pi_{\theta}(x_{0}^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al. (2025)</a></cite> have shown that <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MDM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper bounds of the negative log-likelihood. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose the simple generation algorithm, named the First-Hitting sampler, beyond a continuous time Markov chain (CTMC) that follows the conventional reverse process. First-Hitting sampler randomly selects the index <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for unmasking and samples the next token sequentially as <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{L}\rightarrow{\bm{x}}_{L-1}\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{L}\rightarrow{\bm{x}}_{L-1}\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is proven to converge to <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recent large-scale MDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite> currently adopt time-agnostic modeling and further employ sampling strategies such as the max-confidence beyond the First-Hitting sampler.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Markov transition kernel of random sampler and training hard problem of MDM.</span> Large-scale MDMs first select the masked token to unmask and sample from the categorical distribution. See Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ B.2 Algorithms ‣ Appendix B Training recipe in detail ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Algorithms ‣ Appendix B Training recipe in detail ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> for the detailed sampling algorithm. We here formulate general Markov transition kernels defined by various samplers of MDM.</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{(g)}=\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}{{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{i,(g)}},\;\;\;\;{\bm{x}}_{n-1}\sim\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}[{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{i,(g)}]({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.287em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{(g)}=\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}{{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{i,(g)}},\;\;\;\;{\bm{x}}_{n-1}\sim\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n}[{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{i,(g)}]({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.EGx3.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{i,(g)}]({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{x}})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{cl}g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})\cdot\pi_{\theta}(x^{a^{i}}|{\bm{x}}_{n})&amp;\text{if }{\bm{x}}_{n}^{-{a^{i}}}={\bm{x}}^{-a^{i}},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{i,(g)}]({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{x}})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{cl}g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})\cdot\pi_{\theta}(x^{a^{i}}|{\bm{x}}_{n})&amp;\text{if }{\bm{x}}_{n}^{-{a^{i}}}={\bm{x}}^{-a^{i}},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{-{a^{i}}}=\{x^{1},x^{2},\dots,x^{{a^{i}}-1},x^{{a^{i}}+1},\dots,x^{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{-{a^{i}}}=\{x^{1},x^{2},\dots,x^{{a^{i}}-1},x^{{a^{i}}+1},\dots,x^{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}^{a^{i}}=\mathbb{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}^{a^{i}}=\mathbb{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by definition, and <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x_{n-1}^{a^{i}}|{\bm{x}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x_{n-1}^{a^{i}}|{\bm{x}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the categorical distribution of model prediction of the <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th mask of <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The non-homogeneous Markov kernel for First-Hitting sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for time-agnostic MDMs can be defined as choosing <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n}):=g_{\text{rand}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})=\frac{1}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>rand</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n}):=g_{\text{rand}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})=\frac{1}{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In algorithmic manner, this is equal to first sample <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\sim\mathcal{U}\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\sim\mathcal{U}\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then sample <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{a^{i}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(x^{a^{i}}|{\bm{x}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{a^{i}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(x^{a^{i}}|{\bm{x}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and obtain <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th mask of <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{a^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{a^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\prod_{n=1}^{L}{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{g_{\rm{rand}}})\cdot{\mathbf{P}}_{L}\approx{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\prod_{n=1}^{L}{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{g_{\rm{rand}}})\cdot{\mathbf{P}}_{L}\approx{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is perfectly estimated  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite> have proven that learning <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> for every combination of masked sequence cannot be solved by a polynomial algorithm, and there exist hard subproblems. This can interpreted as there exists some points that true reverse process and model’s reverse process are different, such that resulting in <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\prod_{n=1}^{L}{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{g_{\rm{rand}}})\cdot{\mathbf{P}}_{L}\neq{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\prod_{n=1}^{L}{\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{g_{\rm{rand}}})\cdot{\mathbf{P}}_{L}\neq{\mathbf{P}}_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We provide a simplified example to help understand:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmex1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmex1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmex1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmex1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmex1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmex1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the following simplified zebra (Einstein) puzzle.
There are two houses, and the task is to determine the person living in each house and their favorite food.
The possible persons are <em id="Thmex1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Robert</em> and <em id="Thmex1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Tom</em>, and the possible foods are <em id="Thmex1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">pizza</em> and <em id="Thmex1.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">hamburger</em>.
The clues are given as follows: 1) The person living in the first house is Robert, and 2) Tom likes pizza.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider the MDM directly answer the questions, filling the mask of sentence: <span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“House 1: name: [MASK], food: [MASK], House 2: name: [MASK], food: [MASK]”</span>. Obviously, if MDM tries to answer the name of the person who lives in the first house, the problem becomes trivial. However, if it tries to fill attributes for the second house first before filling attributes of the first house, the problem becomes much harder. As the number of questions and clues grows, this kind of discrepancy would be much larger. Indeed, the zebra puzzle is also known as an NP-hard problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yato &amp; Seta, 2003</a>)</cite>. Note that this is a highly simplified example to help understand the problem of MDM training, and refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite> for a complete explanation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical approach to avoid such hard problems.</span>
Meanwhile, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite> empirically shows that unmasking the token with max-confidence or max-margin helps to avoid such hard problems, leading to better generations. Therefore, in practice, rather than randomly sampling the token for unmasking, large-scale discrete diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite> utilize such rule-based samplings. The weighted kernel related to the max-confidence sampling can be defined with <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n}):=g_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n}):=g_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{\text{conf}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})=\textbf{1}\bigl(a^{i}=\argmax_{a^{j}}(\max_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}^{a^{j}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n}))\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{\text{conf}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})=\textbf{1}\bigl(a^{i}=\argmax_{a^{j}}(\max_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}^{a^{j}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n}))\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">or in other stochastic forms,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{\rm{conf}^{\tau}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>conf</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{\rm{conf}^{\tau}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\exp({\pi_{\theta}(x_{n-1}^{a^{i}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n})/\tau})}{\sum_{a^{j}}\sum_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\exp({\pi_{\theta}(x_{n-1}^{a^{j}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n})/\tau})},\;\;\text{s.t.}\;\lim_{\tau\rightarrow 0+}g_{\rm{conf}^{\tau}}=g_{\text{conf}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>conf</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\exp({\pi_{\theta}(x_{n-1}^{a^{i}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n})/\tau})}{\sum_{a^{j}}\sum_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\exp({\pi_{\theta}(x_{n-1}^{a^{j}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n})/\tau})},\;\;\text{s.t.}\;\lim_{\tau\rightarrow 0+}g_{\rm{conf}^{\tau}}=g_{\text{conf}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{\rm{Top-K}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{\rm{Top-K}}(a^{i}|{\bm{x}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{K}\cdot\textbf{1}(a^{i}\in\underset{{a^{j}}}{\rm{argtopk}}(\max_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}^{a^{j}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n}))),\;\;\text{s.t.}\;g_{\rm{Top-1}}=g_{\rm{conf}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argtopk</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{K}\cdot\textbf{1}(a^{i}\in\underset{{a^{j}}}{\rm{argtopk}}(\max_{c\in\mathcal{V}}\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}^{a^{j}}=c|{\bm{x}}_{n}))),\;\;\text{s.t.}\;g_{\rm{Top-1}}=g_{\rm{conf}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rm{argtopk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argtopk</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rm{argtopk}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the indices from the top-k sampler.
Similarly, the weight kernel for max-margin sampler can be defined with <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{margin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>margin</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{margin}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose value is <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is the mask index with the maximum margin between the first-confident and second-confident token.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While twisted Markov transition kernels driven by heuristic unmasking policies—such as <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm margin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>margin</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm margin}</annotation></semantics></math>—deliver substantial gains, we posit that a stronger policy <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can guide MDM denoising more effectively. Motivated by this, <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we learn <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></span> instead of defining <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in a heuristic manner to find an optimal path. We further cast the learning problem of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> as a KL-regularized MDP, where a strong heuristic sampler serves as a reference policy, enabling stability and policy improvement guarantees.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Existence of optimal path Beyond <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:36%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05725v1/preliminary.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:198/136;" width="198" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Pass@N on GSM8K.
The dashed red line is the single-trajectory accuracy of max-confidence <math id="S3.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test various <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, the mathematical questions benchmark, to probe whether max-confidence is merely strong or whether substantially better unmasking paths exist. We compare three schedulers: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm rand}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm rand}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{Top-K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{Top-K}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic, while <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}</annotation></semantics></math> smoothly interpolates between random and max-confidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each test question, we draw <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent trajectories using either <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm rand}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm rand}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}5}</annotation></semantics></math> and report Pass@N, the fraction of questions for which at least one trajectory is correct.
Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Existence of optimal path Beyond 𝑔_conf ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows three consistent trends:
(i) <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> significantly outperforms <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math>;
(ii) <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}5}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm rand}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm rand}</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting the strength of confidence-based ordering;
(iii) as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> grows, both curves surpass the max-confidence line by a clear margin, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}5}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.92</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.92</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These results carry two implications. First, max-confidence is indeed a strong single-path baseline. Second, the rise of Pass@N above the max-confidence line indicates the presence of higher-yield unmasking trajectories than the deterministic max-confidence order. Brute-force search for such paths is computationally prohibitive and requires unobserved oracle feedback, motivating our learned policy <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> that aims to discover high-reward paths without enumerating many trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the strength of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> and the existence of higher-yield unmasking paths, we set two goals: (i) increase the probability that an MDM produces the correct answer, and (ii) move the induced sampling distribution closer to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm data}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm data}</annotation></semantics></math> than a strong heuristic reference <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore learn a stochastic position-selection policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than fixing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> heuristically. The policy is parameterized as a softmax over masked positions using a learnable scorer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\phi}({\bm{x}})\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\phi}({\bm{x}})\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{\phi}(a^{i}\,|\,{\bm{x}}_{n})=\frac{\exp\!\big([h_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{n})]_{a^{i}}\big)}{\sum_{i^{\prime}=1}^{n}\exp\!\big([h_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{n})]_{a^{i^{\prime}}}\big)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{\phi}(a^{i}\,|\,{\bm{x}}_{n})=\frac{\exp\!\big([h_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{n})]_{a^{i}}\big)}{\sum_{i^{\prime}=1}^{n}\exp\!\big([h_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{n})]_{a^{i^{\prime}}}\big)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We cast unmasking as a regularized Markov decision process (MDP) with verifiable terminal rewards. Each episode begins from a prompt–answer pair where the answer is fully masked, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[{\bm{x}}_{L}:{\mathbf{q}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bm{x}}_{L}:{\mathbf{q}}]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> referring to the answer distribution and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{L}=\mathbb{M}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{L}=\mathbb{M}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and terminates when all masks are removed at <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}\in\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{V}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}\in\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{V}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. At state <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the action space is the set of masked indices <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{{\bm{x}}_{n}}=\{a^{1}[{\bm{x}}_{n}],\dots,a^{n}[{\bm{x}}_{n}]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{{\bm{x}}_{n}}=\{a^{1}[{\bm{x}}_{n}],\dots,a^{n}[{\bm{x}}_{n}]\}</annotation></semantics></math>; the policy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{\phi}(\cdot\,|\,{\bm{x}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}(\cdot\,|\,{\bm{x}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> selects one index <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}\in\mathcal{A}_{{\bm{x}}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}\in\mathcal{A}_{{\bm{x}}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> to unmask. Transitions factor through the fixed MDM denoiser <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})={\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{x}}_{n-1})=g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})\cdot\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n},a_{n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})={\mathbf{T}}_{n}^{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{x}}_{n-1})=g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})\cdot\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n},a_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n},a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n},a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the token distribution for position <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The agent thus controls <em id="S3.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> to unmask, while the environment dynamics are induced by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Rewards are verifiable: an external checker evaluates the fully unmasked output <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and returns <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To stabilize and accelerate training, we introduce a strong <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reference policy</em> <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Top-<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) and optimize a theoretical KL-regularized GRPO-style objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh, 2025</a>)</cite>. We optimize directly over the <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">terminal-output distribution</em>
induced by the policy:
all expectations are taken with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the regularizer compares
terminal-output marginals.
Accordingly, our theoretical loss is an <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">output-level</em> GRPO objective without clipping:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\phi}\;\;\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}}\Biggl[\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\!\biggl[\frac{p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}{p_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\,A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\biggr]-\beta\,D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\,\big\|\,p_{g_{\mathrm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\right)\Biggr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\phi}\;\;\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}}\Biggl[\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\!\biggl[\frac{p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}{p_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\,A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\biggr]-\beta\,D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\,\big\|\,p_{g_{\mathrm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\right)\Biggr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is a standardized advantage. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{g}({\mathbf{q}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}[r({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})]\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g}({\mathbf{q}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}[r({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})]\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{std}_{g}({\mathbf{q}})=\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}[(r({\bm{x}}_{0})-r_{g}({\mathbf{q}}))^{2}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>std</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{std}_{g}({\mathbf{q}})=\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}[(r({\bm{x}}_{0})-r_{g}({\mathbf{q}}))^{2}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})=(r({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})-r_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\mathbf{q}}))/(\mathrm{std}_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\mathbf{q}})+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>std</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})=(r({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})-r_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\mathbf{q}}))/(\mathrm{std}_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\mathbf{q}})+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This objective follows the <em id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">output-level</em> GRPO loss used in DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> rather than the original <em id="S3.SS2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">token-wise</em> GRPO used in DeepSeekMath <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
The regularizer keeps <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math>, acting as a trust-region that mitigates instability while enabling improvement over a strong baseline; selecting an appropriate <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a high-accuracy starting point and accelerates convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Theoretical analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide how <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> optimizing Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) can guarantee the convergence of the policy model and superior performance over the reference policy <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh (2025)</a></cite> have proven that a policy model learning output-level GRPO loss (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>)) can converge to its optimal performance <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is higher than <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Restatement of GRPO convergence theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh, 2025</a>)</cite>)<span id="Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&gt;r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&gt;r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define for <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}(r_{g})=\frac{1}{1+\frac{1-r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}}{r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}}\exp\bigr(-\frac{1}{\beta}\frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{g}(1-r_{g})+\epsilon}}\bigl)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub></mfrac><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}(r_{g})=\frac{1}{1+\frac{1-r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}}{r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}}\exp\bigr(-\frac{1}{\beta}\frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{g}(1-r_{g})+\epsilon}}\bigl)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for the local optimizer <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{n}=\max_{\phi}\;\rm{Eq.~(\ref{eq:theoretical_loss})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Eq</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.497em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext><a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{n}=\max_{\phi}\;\rm{Eq.~(\ref{eq:theoretical_loss})}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\rm{old}}=\phi_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\rm{old}}=\phi_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probability of success satisfies the following fixed point iteration i.e we have almost surely for all <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{g_{\phi_{n}}}({\mathbf{q}})=h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}(r_{g_{\phi_{n-1}}}({\mathbf{q}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{g_{\phi_{n}}}({\mathbf{q}})=h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}(r_{g_{\phi_{n-1}}}({\mathbf{q}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g_{\phi_{0}}}({\mathbf{q}})=r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g_{\phi_{0}}}({\mathbf{q}})=r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed point of <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that have <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}^{\prime}(r_{g^{*}})|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}^{\prime}(r_{g^{*}})|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Given that <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\epsilon,p_{\rm{ref}}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous in <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, following local fixed point convergence is established:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{n\rightarrow{\infty}}r_{g_{\phi_{n}}}=r_{g^{*}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{n\rightarrow{\infty}}r_{g_{\phi_{n}}}=r_{g^{*}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g^{*}}&gt;r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g^{*}}&gt;r_{g_{\rm{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, setting <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a strong proxy model is important, as we can always get a better policy <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, in the following we prove that policy model <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> maximizing Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) can restore <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> better than <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reference-KL Tightening for MDM Policy Improvement)<span id="Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume there exists ideal MDM <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}(\theta^{*})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MDM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}(\theta^{*})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> where data is composed of prompt and oracle answer <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathbf{q}},{\mathbf{a}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbf{q}},{\mathbf{a}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r({\mathbf{q}},{\mathbf{a}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r({\mathbf{q}},{\mathbf{a}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Now consider incomplete MDM <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}(\theta)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MDM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{MDM}}(\theta)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies Eq. (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Theorem 1 (Restatement of GRPO convergence theorem ( , )). ‣ 3.3 Theoretical analysis ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) for <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Let let <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a policy model such that, for every <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta^{*}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta^{*}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<em id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">i.e</em>.<span id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{supp}(p_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}}))\subseteq\operatorname{supp}(p_{g_{\mathrm{ref}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{supp}(p_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}}))\subseteq\operatorname{supp}(p_{g_{\mathrm{ref}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the following inequality holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{\theta^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\phi^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}}))\leq D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{\theta^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{\theta^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\phi^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}}))\leq D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{\theta^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Or equivalently,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{data}}({\mathbf{a}}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\phi^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}}))\leq D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{data}}({\mathbf{a}}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{data}}({\mathbf{a}}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\phi^{*}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}}))\leq D_{\rm{KL}}(p_{\rm{data}}({\mathbf{a}}|{\mathbf{q}})||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh (2025)</a></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text"> provides policy dynamic under training Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Using the result given in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (GRPO Policy Dynamic ( , )). ‣ Proof. ‣ C.1 proof of KL tightening for MDM policy ‣ Appendix C Detailed Proof ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Appendix, we can express <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and thereby derive KL divergence. Please refer to Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 proof of KL tightening for MDM policy ‣ Appendix C Detailed Proof ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> for detailed proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our provided Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2 (Reference-KL Tightening for MDM Policy Improvement). ‣ 3.3 Theoretical analysis ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> guarantees the closer sampling to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\rm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\rm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> or ideal MDM <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, under the assumptions of two theorems, initializing with <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi_{0}}=g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi_{0}}=g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> and repeatedly optimizing <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) yields a convergent policy <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> that (i) attains a higher expected reward than <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> and (ii) generates samples that are closer to the real data distribution than those from <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Practical realization of unmasking policy optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we provide practical and tractable training objectives with various <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In a real-world setting where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|</annotation></semantics></math> are high, Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is not directly learnable since <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is intractable in MDMs. It requires marginalizing over every trajectory to <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})=\sum_{{\bm{x}}_{1},...,{\bm{x}}_{L}}p_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{0:L}|{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})=\sum_{{\bm{x}}_{1},...,{\bm{x}}_{L}}p_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{0:L}|{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, making both the main objective (reward maximization) and the KL term in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) intractable. To address this, we provide a surrogate loss that can alternate the main objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Output–Token Level Gradient Alignment (informal))<span id="Thmprop1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For MDM <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and unmasking policy model <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})=g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})\cdot\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n},a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})=g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})\cdot\pi_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{n-1}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n},a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})=\sum_{{\bm{x}}_{1},...,{\bm{x}}_{L}}p_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{0:L}|{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})=\sum_{{\bm{x}}_{1},...,{\bm{x}}_{L}}p_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{0:L}|{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the output level loss <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>output</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}</annotation></semantics></math> and token-wise policy loss <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>token</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>output</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\bigr[\frac{p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}{p_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\bigl]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\bigr[\frac{p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}{p_{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}\mid{\mathbf{q}})}A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\bigl],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>token</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}}\mathbb{E}_{\{a_{1:L},{\bm{x}}_{0:L}\}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\bigr[\sum_{n=1}^{L}\frac{g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})}{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})}A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\bigl]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{\mathcal{Q}}}\mathbb{E}_{\{a_{1:L},{\bm{x}}_{0:L}\}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{q}})}\bigr[\sum_{n=1}^{L}\frac{g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})}{g_{\phi_{\rm old}}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})}A({\mathbf{q}},{\bm{x}}_{0})\bigl].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that we optimize <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> carefully such that <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\approx\phi_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\approx\phi_{old}</annotation></semantics></math>, the two gradients are approximately equal:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}\approx\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>token</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>output</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}\approx\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}=\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>token</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>output</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{token}}=\nabla_{\phi}\mathcal{L}_{\rm{output}}</annotation></semantics></math> at first optimization step where <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\phi_{\rm{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\phi_{\rm{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Although the result can be readily inferred from standard RL, we provide a rigorous proof for our setting: sparse-reward, finite-horizon, episodic, and non-stationary MDPs where we maximize a GRPO-style objective.
Please refer to Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Surrogate loss of output-level reward maximization ‣ Appendix C Detailed Proof ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a> for the full proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05725v1/model.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/104;" width="384" height="104"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The structure of our unmasking policy model. The model is composed of a single Transformer layer and a 3-layer MLP. In inference time, the model proceeds as follows: 1) Given an input sentence, we run the base MDMs transformer and extract a feature. 2) This feature branches into two paths: first, the base MDMs continue to produce a prediction of token distribution, and second, fed to the policy model’s transformer layer. 3) We concatenate the extracted feature with the base MDM’s Top-K probabilities. 4) The concatenated feature is then processed by a 3-layer MLP to yield a policy over unmasking positions. The shared structure with frozen MDM supports memory-efficient training (<span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, LLaDA: 8B, policy model: 134M), where we only update the unmasking policy model.
The details and memory-efficient training algorithm are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Algorithms ‣ Appendix B Training recipe in detail ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While Eq. (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Proposition 1 (Output–Token Level Gradient Alignment (informal)). ‣ 3.4 Practical realization of unmasking policy optimization ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) optimizes <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})=\sum_{{\bm{x}}_{1},...,{\bm{x}}_{L}}p_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{0:L}|{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})=\sum_{{\bm{x}}_{1},...,{\bm{x}}_{L}}p_{\phi}({\bm{x}}_{0:L}|{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is intractable, our surrogate loss in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Proposition 1 (Output–Token Level Gradient Alignment (informal)). ‣ 3.4 Practical realization of unmasking policy optimization ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) optimizes <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})/g_{\phi_{\rm old}}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})/g_{\phi_{\rm old}}(a_{n}\mid{\bm{x}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> which is tractable once trajectory is sampled.
Now, we detail how we train the policy model <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> efficiently. Our unmasking policy model structure is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.4 Practical realization of unmasking policy optimization ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and the full training algorithm table can be found in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Algorithms ‣ Appendix B Training recipe in detail ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final Training Objective.</span>
By using Proposition <a href="#Thmprop1" title="Proposition 1 (Output–Token Level Gradient Alignment (informal)). ‣ 3.4 Practical realization of unmasking policy optimization ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, clipping, and Monte Carlo approximation, we transform the theoretical loss (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Reformulating the problem as reinforcement learning ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>)) into tractable unmasking policy optimization (UPO) loss as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{UPO}}(g_{\phi},g_{\rm{ref}},D)=\mathbb{E}_{{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{Q}},\{{\bm{x}}_{0:L}^{(g)},a_{1:L}^{(g)}\}_{g=1}^{G}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}}}\Biggl[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{g}^{G}\Biggl(\Bigl(\frac{1}{L}\sum_{n=1}^{L}\min\bigl(\frac{g_{\phi}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})}{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})}A_{g}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>UPO</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>G</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\rm{UPO}}(g_{\phi},g_{\rm{ref}},D)=\mathbb{E}_{{{\mathbf{q}}\sim\rho_{Q}},\{{\bm{x}}_{0:L}^{(g)},a_{1:L}^{(g)}\}_{g=1}^{G}\sim p_{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}}}\Biggl[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{g}^{G}\Biggl(\Bigl(\frac{1}{L}\sum_{n=1}^{L}\min\bigl(\frac{g_{\phi}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})}{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})}A_{g},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{clip}(\frac{g_{\phi}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})}{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)A_{g}\bigr)\Bigr)-\beta\cdot D(p_{g_{\phi}}||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}})\Biggr)\Biggr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{clip}(\frac{g_{\phi}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})}{g_{\phi_{\rm{old}}}(a_{n}^{(g)}|{\bm{x}}_{n}^{(g)})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)A_{g}\bigr)\Bigr)-\beta\cdot D(p_{g_{\phi}}||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}})\Biggr)\Biggr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(p_{g_{\phi}}||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(p_{g_{\phi}}||p_{g_{\rm{ref}}})</annotation></semantics></math> should be empirical and tractable estimator of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\,\|\,p_{g_{\mathrm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{g_{\phi}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\,\|\,p_{g_{\mathrm{ref}}}({\bm{x}}_{0}|{\mathbf{q}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and clipped loss has been proven to attain global optimality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Please note that <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g)</annotation></semantics></math> in superscript of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In 3.4 Practical realization of unmasking policy optimization ‣ 3 Improving Unmasking Policy Beyond a Reference Policy ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) is the group index.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Various realization of <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{UPO}}(g_{\phi},g_{\rm{ref}},D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>UPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{UPO}}(g_{\phi},g_{\rm{ref}},D)</annotation></semantics></math> varying <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:515.5pt;height:98.2pt;vertical-align:-46.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Reference policy</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:111.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Max-Confidence</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S3.T1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:127.2pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Softmax Realization</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S3.T1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:127.2pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<span id="S3.T1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">
