</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines and setup.</span>
We compare our learned unmasking policy with four widely used schedulers: random <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, margin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. For <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Zebra</span>, we utilize max-confidence realization, and for <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Math500</span>, we use Top-<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> realization where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Main result ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that our policy matches or surpasses all heuristics on every benchmark. For <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Zebra</span>, where we use <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> as the reference, our model exceeds this reference: <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span> improves from 70.5% to 81.7%, and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Zebra</span> from 33.7% to 36.2%. On <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span>, trained with a Top-<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> reference, our policy reaches 70.3% and exceeds the strong max-confidence baseline at 68.4%. On <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Math500</span>, also trained with a Top-<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> reference, our policy attains 28.4%, tying the best baseline while improving over max-confidence at 27.2%. Overall, max-confidence remains a strong single-path heuristic, yet a learned policy trained in our regularized framework consistently surpasses it.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Accuracy on <span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span> of post-trained LLaDA with diffu-GRPO by sampling strategy.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:1051.7pt;height:35.8pt;vertical-align:-15.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S4.T3.4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Random</span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Margin</span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Entropy</span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.3.1.2" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Confidence</span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.4.1.2" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;">Ours</span></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.638" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.638</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.638</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.750</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.750</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.740" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.740</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.740</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.751" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.751</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.751</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.4.1.1.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.764</span></span></span>
</span></span></p>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05725v1/ablation.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/113;" width="548" height="113"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Mean and standard deviation of group reward during training under <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm UPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>UPO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm UPO}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SUDOKU</span>, we compare pretrained policy trained with/without CE to <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for
<span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span>, we compare randomly initialized <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi_{\rm Top\text{-}K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi_{\rm Top\text{-}K}}</annotation></semantics></math> trained with/without KL to <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compatibility with diffu-GRPO.</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduces diffu-GRPO, an RL method that post-trains the MDM itself via GRPO. Our approach also uses RL but targets a different component: we optimize the unmasking policy while keeping the base MDM fixed. The two methods are therefore complementary and can be combined. To demonstrate this, we first post-train <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLaDA-8B-Instruct</span> on <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span> with diffu-GRPO, then train our policy model with Top-<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> realization, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm{UPO}}(g_{\phi_{\rm{Top-5}}},g_{\rm{Top-5}},\mathcal{L}_{\rm{KL}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>UPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm{UPO}}(g_{\phi_{\rm{Top-5}}},g_{\rm{Top-5}},\mathcal{L}_{\rm{KL}})</annotation></semantics></math>. As reported in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, our unmasking policy model yields additional gains on top of the diffu-GRPO model: +12.6% over random and +1.3% over max-confidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Power of regularization.</span>
We compare the mean and standard deviation of the group reward while optimizing <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm UPO}(g_{\phi},g_{\rm ref},D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>UPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm UPO}(g_{\phi},g_{\rm ref},D)</annotation></semantics></math> in two setups:
for <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span>, we train a pretrained policy with/without CE to <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span>, we train a randomly initialized <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi_{\rm Top\text{-}K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi_{\rm Top\text{-}K}}</annotation></semantics></math> with/without <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm KL}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, adding the divergence term yields higher final accuracy and, at similar mean rewards—even near 1—maintains a larger group-reward standard deviation. This helps the policy model avoid premature convergence under the GRPO framework and reach a higher convergence point.
We conjecture that, for <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rm KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rm KL}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm Top\text{-}K}</annotation></semantics></math>  force the probability of the trajectory sampled from <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi_{\rm{Top-K}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi_{\rm{Top-K}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be all equal and prevent early path collapse.
For <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span>, we suppose that CE toward <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>conf</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> counteracts collapse onto a suboptimal order by pulling the policy back to high-confidence alternatives, keeping multiple competitive paths active and maintaining variance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced a KL-regularized MDP formulation for learning the unmasking policy in masked diffusion models. Our analysis provides two guarantees: convergence to a fixed point that improves expected reward over the reference policy, and a KL tightening showing that the terminal-output distribution is closer to the real data than the reference. To make the framework practical, we derived a tractable surrogate loss and proposed several reference realizations. Across <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span>, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Zebra</span>, <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span>, and <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Math500</span>, the learned unmasking policy model consistently matches or surpasses strong heuristics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Future directions.</span>
On <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sudoku</span>, max-confidence exceeds Top-<math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, suggesting that near-optimal training can benefit from occasional low-confidence selections. Meanwhile, on <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GSM8K</span>, the much larger search space makes Top-<math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> preferable for stability. In future work, we plan to develop an unmasking policy optimization algorithm that explores the full mask tokens even when the search space is large.</p>
</div>
</section>
